WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.502
Here’s how to run “Choose how to decide”.

00:00:05.552 --> 00:00:07.504
It takes about forty minutes.

00:00:07.554 --> 00:00:13.684
Start by asking everyone to write decisions that come up again and again in the team. One per post-it.

00:00:13.734 --> 00:00:21.222
Things like: what we work on next, who joins which project, how we spend the budget, when we say no to a request.

00:00:21.272 --> 00:00:25.670
Put them up, cluster the similar ones, and pick one that matters to the team.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.176
Choose one that causes some friction today.

00:00:28.226 --> 00:00:30.982
That way the result is immediately useful.

00:00:31.032 --> 00:00:33.832
Now read the twelve ways to decide together.

00:00:33.882 --> 00:00:36.241
For each group, ask one question.

00:00:36.291 --> 00:00:40.112
Does this decision need narrowing down? Does it need speed?

00:00:40.162 --> 00:00:41.840
Does it need everyone on board?

00:00:41.889 --> 00:00:44.474
Or does it need a clear final say?

00:00:44.524 --> 00:00:46.973
Then choose one method for this decision.

00:00:47.023 --> 00:00:53.454
Write it on the canvas, together with who decides, who should be asked first, and who should be told afterwards.

00:00:53.504 --> 00:01:00.289
Here’s an example. “What we work on next month: the team proposes, our product owner decides by consent.

00:01:00.339 --> 00:01:02.860
We ask our two main stakeholders before.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.230
We tell everyone in the planning meeting.”

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.270
Short, clear, and anyone can check it.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.108
If you have time, do a second or third decision.

00:01:11.158 --> 00:01:14.141
But one good one is better than five vague ones.

00:01:14.191 --> 00:01:20.008
And finally, agree to try it the next few times the decision comes up, then talk about how it felt.

00:01:20.058 --> 00:01:23.021
Like the rhythm, a decision method is an experiment.

00:01:23.071 --> 00:01:24.504
You keep it if it works.

